<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/10th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Goryeo–Khitan War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/993">993</a>–<a href="/years/1019">1019</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Goryeo–Khitan wars · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Goryeo–Khitan War was a series of invasions of the Korean kingdom of Goryeo by the Khitan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a> between 993 and 1019. Goryeo retained land south of the Yalu by diplomacy in 993 and resisted a major invasion in 1010, which burned the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaesong">Kaesong</a>. The Goryeo general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gang-gam-chan">Gang Gam-chan</a> destroyed the last Khitan army at Kwiju in 1019, after which peace followed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/goryeo-khitan-war.jpg" alt="Goryeo–Khitan War" width="320" height="235" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 玖巧仔 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Goryeo%E2%80%93Khitan_Liao_War.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 926 the Khitan-led Liao dynasty conquered Balhae, and its last crown prince and many of its people fled south to Goryeo. Taejo of Goryeo welcomed the refugees as kin and pursued a policy of expansion to the north. Relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khitans">Khitans</a> were cold: in 942 Goryeo refused a gift of 50 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>, banished the Khitan envoy to an island and let the camels starve under a bridge.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 993 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> invaded the northwestern frontier with a force its commander claimed was 800,000 strong. After a stalemate, Goryeo agreed to end relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a>, pay tribute to the Liao and adopt their calendar, and through the talks of the diplomat Sŏ Hŭi the land up to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalu-river">Yalu River</a>, then held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> tribes, was accepted as Goryeo's on the ground that it had once belonged to Goguryeo. Goryeo nonetheless kept in touch with the Song and built fortresses in the new territory.</p><p>After General Kang Cho killed King Mokjong in a coup in 1009, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> attacked in 1010 with 400,000 troops, claiming to avenge him. Kang Cho was defeated and executed, and King Hyeonjong fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naju">Naju</a> while the capital was sacked and burnt. The Liao withdrew, but the court refused the emperor's demand that the king come in person and cede border areas, and indecisive attacks followed in 1015, 1016 and 1017.</p><p>In 1018 an army of 100,000 invaded. Kang Kam-ch'an dammed a stream east of Heunghwajin and released it on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> as they crossed, then attacked with 12,000 horsemen; Kang Min-cheom destroyed 10,000 of the retreating troops, and at Gwiju barely a few thousand survived.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> gathered another army, but neither side could win outright. In 1020 Hyeonjong resumed tribute, and in 1022 the Liao recognised his reign and sent an envoy to invest him. The peace of 1022 required only that the king acknowledge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a> to the Liao and free detained Liao envoys; Goryeo sent no embassy to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, apart from one in 1030, until 1070, and relations with the Liao stayed peaceful until that dynasty's end. The historian Bielenstein, however, held that the two states made peace as equals.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Goryeo–Khitan War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
